# Raw iron from iron ore or deepslate iron ore (`#iron_ores` is the 26.3 block
# tag that holds both), so one rule covers a surface vein and the deepslate
# body a tunnel opens.
#
# Iron ore needs a stone pickaxe or better: a wooden (or golden) pickaxe breaks
# the block and drops nothing. The skill therefore refuses before it walks,
# `fail(needs_tool)`, the same shape `get_iron_ingot` uses for `no_raw_iron`:
# an honest kind the caller can act on beats an ore that vanishes. The guard
# names the four tiers above wood on purpose. Golden is left out because a gold
# pickaxe has the wooden harvest level, and copper is left out because this
# repo's tool table does not rank it; being wrong there costs one rung, since
# the caller then gets a stone pickaxe first.
#
# Why it does not fetch the pickaxe itself (the admitted shape was
# `do get_stone_pickaxe()` when missing, and that call cannot load): a stone
# pickaxe is four frames by itself (get_stone_pickaxe -> get_cobblestone ->
# get_wooden_pickaxe -> get_planks), and the core cap is four frames root
# included, so calling it from here is a five-frame chain the loader rejects
# and the interpreter would refuse at run time. Inlining the pickaxe here is
# the other way out, and it does not fit either: three cobblestone, two sticks,
# a placed table and the craft are four rules on top of this skill's four, and
# every combination under six rules leaves a state with no true rule (three
# cobblestone and one stick, or the ingredients ready and no table) where the
# body would spin or die with `no_rule`. So the rung is cumulative like the
# rest of the ladder: a body that got this far has a stone pickaxe from the
# previous rung, and a body that does not is told `needs_tool` in one tick.
#
# Why the rules are ordered this way: the goal check is on top. The tool guard
# comes before the search so no step is spent on an ore that cannot drop. The
# mine rule commits with `until count(raw_iron) >= $n`, so one call keeps
# mining while the goal is short and releases the moment it is met. The last
# rule is the search: `find_ore` walks the surface, then tunnels with
# `branch_mine`, and its failure (normally not_found) is this skill's failure
# after the usual three tries.
skill get_raw_iron(n: int = 1)
  doc "Have n raw iron, mined from iron ore or deepslate iron ore with a stone pickaxe or better; fails needs_tool without one."
  rules:
    count(raw_iron) >= $n -> done
    not (has(stone_pickaxe) or has(iron_pickaxe) or has(diamond_pickaxe) or has(netherite_pickaxe)) -> fail(needs_tool)
    near(#iron_ores, 5) -> mine(#iron_ores) until count(raw_iron) >= $n
    true -> do find_ore(iron_ore)
